A = [
dict(n=14, slug="transformer", title="What Is the \"T\" in GPT?", topic="Transformer",
buzz='GPT stands for Generative Pre-trained Transformer. Most people know the first two words. The third is the one that changed everything.',
hw='Old AI read a sentence like someone reading through a keyhole: one word at a time, left to right, forgetting the start by the end.\n\nA **transformer** lays the whole sentence out on a table and lets every word look at every other word at once (that\'s attention, write-up 13). That\'s the hello world: read everything together, not one word at a time.',
step='A transformer is a stack of layers. In each layer, every token (write-up 10) gathers information from the others through attention, then refines it. Dozens of layers later, the model has a rich picture of the text and predicts the next token.\n\nBecause all words are processed together, transformers train well on GPUs (write-up 46), which is why they could be scaled to today\'s huge models. The design was published by Google researchers in 2017 in a paper called "Attention Is All You Need".',
met='Every major chatbot, translator and coding assistant. Many image, speech and video models use transformers too.',
name='The **transformer** architecture.',
words=[("Architecture","the overall design of a model."),("Layer","one round of attention and refinement."),("GPT","Generative Pre-trained Transformer.")],
notit='A transformer isn\'t a specific product or a robot. It\'s a design, like "suspension bridge" is a design, used by many different models.',
take=["Read \"transformer\" as \"the design behind modern AI\".","Remember why long inputs cost more: every word looks at every other word.","Expect new designs to challenge it; none has replaced it yet."],
close="Read the whole sentence at once. That's a transformer.",
q="Did you know what the T in GPT stood for?",
scene='Old AI read through a keyhole: one word at a time.\nBy the end of the sentence, it forgot the start.',
hook="A transformer lays the whole sentence out and reads it all at once.",
extra="It's the T in GPT."),

dict(n=17, slug="prompt", title="Why Does the Way You Ask Change the Answer?", topic="Prompt",
buzz='"Just write a better prompt." Everyone says it. What exactly is a prompt?',
hw='Ask a new colleague "Write something about our product." You\'ll get something vague. Ask "Write a 3-line email to a busy CFO explaining how our product cuts invoice processing time, friendly tone." You\'ll get something useful.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: a **prompt** is everything you give the AI to work with, and it shapes everything you get back.',
step='Remember write-up 11: an AI continues text in the most likely way. Your prompt is the start of that text. A clear prompt makes the right answer the most likely continuation.\n\nGood prompts usually say who it\'s for, what you want, the format, and any examples or limits. Pasted documents, earlier chat messages and hidden instructions are part of the prompt too.',
met='Every message you type into a chatbot. The text box in image generators.',
name='A **prompt**.',
words=[("Prompt","the input you give an AI."),("Instruction","the part that says what to do."),("Output","what the AI gives back.")],
notit='A prompt isn\'t a magic spell or a secret code. It\'s a clear brief. And a great prompt can\'t fix missing information; if the AI doesn\'t have the facts, give them.',
take=["Say who it's for, what you want, and the format.","Give an example of a good answer.","Add the facts it needs instead of hoping it knows them."],
close="Ask like you're briefing a smart new colleague.",
q="What's the best prompt tip you've learned?",
scene='"Write something about our product."\nYou get something vague.',
hook="A prompt is your brief to the AI. Vague brief, vague answer.",
extra="Say who it's for, what you want, and the format."),

dict(n=18, slug="prompt-engineering", title="Is Prompt Engineering a Real Skill?", topic="Prompt engineering",
buzz='"Prompt engineer" became a job title, then a joke, then quietly part of every AI job. Is it real engineering?',
hw='One person asks the AI 50 questions and copies the answers that look fine. Another writes the instructions once, tests them on 50 real cases, fixes what fails, and saves the version that works.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **prompt engineering** is designing, testing and improving prompts (write-up 17) systematically, not just typing better questions.',
step='In real products, prompts are reused thousands of times. So teams write them carefully: clear role, steps, format, examples (write-up 21), and rules for unclear cases. Then they test them against a set of real examples (write-up 88), change one thing at a time, and keep versions.\n\nAs models get better at understanding plain requests, the tricks matter less and the clear thinking matters more.',
met='Any AI feature in an app you use: someone wrote and tested the prompt behind it.',
name='**Prompt engineering**.',
words=[("Prompt template","a reusable prompt with blanks for each case."),("Iteration","changing one thing, then testing again."),("Prompt version","a saved, tested copy of a prompt.")],
notit='It isn\'t finding magic words, and it can\'t make a model do what it\'s unable to do. For hard rules and guarantees, use code, not prompts (Series 3).',
take=["Test prompts on real examples, not one lucky try.","Change one thing at a time and keep versions.","Put must-never-break rules in code, not in the prompt."],
close="Not magic words. Clear briefs, tested.",
q="Do you test your prompts, or try until one looks right?",
scene='One person tries prompts until an answer looks fine.\nAnother tests one prompt on 50 real cases.',
hook="Prompt engineering is the second person.",
extra="Design, test, fix, keep versions."),

dict(n=19, slug="system-prompt", title="What's the Hidden Instruction Behind Every Chatbot?", topic="System prompt",
buzz='"System prompt." Companies guard theirs. Hackers try to leak them. What\'s in it?',
hw='Before a new support agent takes their first call, the manager tells them: "You work for Acme. Be friendly. Never promise refunds. Answer only about our products."\n\nCustomers never hear that briefing, but it shapes every answer. That\'s the hello world: a **system prompt** is the hidden briefing given to the AI before you type anything.',
step='Every chat app sends the AI a system prompt first, then your message. It sets the role, tone, format, what to avoid and sometimes today\'s date or tools it can use.\n\nThe AI gives it extra weight, but it isn\'t unbreakable: clever user messages can sometimes push against it (write-ups 85 and 86). So real rules, like refund limits, belong in code too.',
met='Every branded chatbot that stays "in character". Custom GPTs and assistants you set up with instructions.',
name='The **system prompt**, also called **system instructions**.',
words=[("System prompt","hidden setup instructions sent before the conversation."),("User prompt","what the person types."),("Persona","the role and tone the AI is told to take.")],
notit='A system prompt isn\'t a security wall or a secret vault. Don\'t put passwords or confidential data in it, and don\'t rely on it alone to enforce rules.',
take=["Write your assistant's role, tone and limits in the system prompt.","Never put secrets in it.","Enforce money, access and safety rules in code as well."],
close="The briefing the customer never hears.",
q="If you built a chatbot, what would its first instruction be?",
scene='Before the first call, the manager briefs the new agent:\n"Be friendly. Never promise refunds."',
hook="A system prompt is that briefing, given to the AI before you type.",
extra="Hidden, important, and not a security wall."),

dict(n=20, slug="zero-shot", title="Can an AI Do a Task It Was Never Shown?", topic="Zero-shot",
buzz='"Zero-shot." It appears in research papers and product launches. It sounds like a missed basketball throw.',
hw='Ask an AI: "Is this review positive or negative? \'The battery died in an hour.\'" You gave no examples, no training, just the question. It answers "negative".\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **zero-shot** means asking an AI to do a task with **zero examples**, relying only on what it already learned.',
step='Older AI needed thousands of labelled examples for each task (write-up 3). Large models learned so much general language that they can follow a plain instruction for many new tasks straight away.\n\nZero-shot works well for common, clear tasks. For unusual formats or subtle judgement calls, adding a few examples (write-up 21) usually helps.',
met='Asking a chatbot to translate, classify or summarise something without showing it how.',
name='**Zero-shot** prompting.',
words=[("Shot","an example included in the prompt."),("Zero-shot","no examples, just the instruction."),("Generalisation","handling tasks it wasn't specifically trained on.")],
notit='Zero-shot doesn\'t mean zero knowledge. The model learned from huge amounts of text. And it\'s not always reliable for niche tasks.',
take=["Try zero-shot first; it's the simplest.","If results wobble, add two or three examples.","Test on real cases before trusting it at scale."],
close="No examples, just ask.",
q="Do you usually give the AI examples, or just ask?",
scene='"Is this review positive or negative? The battery died in an hour."\nNo examples given.',
hook="The AI answers \"negative\" anyway. That's zero-shot.",
extra="Zero examples, just the instruction."),

dict(n=21, slug="few-shot", title="Why Do Examples Help an AI So Much?", topic="Few-shot",
buzz='"Few-shot prompting." A simple trick that often beats long instructions.',
hw='You want product names turned into short codes. Instead of explaining the rules, you show:\n"Blue Cotton Shirt → BLU-SHT"\n"Red Wool Scarf → RED-SCF"\n"Green Linen Trousers → ?"\n\nThe AI answers "GRN-TRS". That\'s the hello world: **few-shot** means showing a few examples in the prompt so the AI copies the pattern.',
step='Examples show format, tone and edge cases faster than words can describe them. Two to five good examples usually do the job.\n\nChoose them carefully: the AI copies everything, including mistakes and accidental patterns. If all examples are short, answers will be short.',
met='Prompt templates that include "Example 1, Example 2". Spreadsheet AI features that learn from the first few rows you fill in.',
name='**Few-shot** prompting, also called **in-context learning**.',
words=[("Few-shot","a handful of examples in the prompt."),("In-context learning","learning a pattern from the prompt, without retraining."),("Edge case","an unusual example worth showing.")],
notit='Few-shot doesn\'t retrain or change the model. It only guides this one answer. Remove the examples, and the effect is gone.',
take=["Show 2 to 5 examples instead of long rules.","Include one tricky case among them.","Check your examples are correct; they will be copied."],
close="Show, don't explain.",
q="Have examples ever fixed a prompt for you?",
scene='"Blue Cotton Shirt: BLU-SHT. Red Wool Scarf: RED-SCF.\nGreen Linen Trousers: ?"',
hook="The AI answers GRN-TRS. That's few-shot: show a few examples, it copies the pattern.",
extra="Often better than a page of rules."),

dict(n=22, slug="chain-of-thought", title="Why Does \"Think Step by Step\" Work?", topic="Chain of thought",
buzz='"Chain of thought." Adding "let\'s think step by step" to a prompt famously made AIs better at maths. Why would that work?',
hw='Ask: "A shop sells pens at 3 for ₹20. How much for 12?" Blurt an answer and you might get it wrong. Write the steps: 12 pens is 4 groups of 3, 4 × ₹20 = ₹80.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **chain of thought** means the AI writes its reasoning steps before the final answer.',
step='Remember write-up 11: an AI writes one token at a time. Each written step becomes part of the text it builds on, so it can use its own working, like scribbling on paper.\n\nResearchers found in 2022 that simply asking models to "think step by step" improved results. Reasoning models (write-up 71) are now trained to do this automatically, often in a hidden "thinking" section.',
met='Chatbots that show their working on maths problems. "Thinking" sections in newer AI apps.',
name='**Chain-of-thought** prompting.',
words=[("Chain of thought","step-by-step reasoning written before the answer."),("Intermediate step","one part of the working."),("Reasoning","working something out rather than recalling it.")],
notit='The written steps aren\'t always the real reason the AI chose its answer, and steps can look logical while containing a mistake. Check the answer, not just the working.',
take=["Ask for steps on maths, logic and planning tasks.","Skip it for simple look-ups; it just costs more tokens.","Check the final answer independently when it matters."],
close="Write the working. Then the answer.",
q="Do you ask AI to show its working?",
scene='Pens are 3 for ₹20. How much for 12?\nBlurt it, or work it out?',
hook="Writing the steps first helps an AI too. That's chain of thought.",
extra="Its own working becomes something it can build on."),

dict(n=23, slug="context-engineering", title="Is Context Engineering the New Prompt Engineering?", topic="Context engineering",
buzz='"Context engineering" became a buzzword in 2025. Some say it replaces prompt engineering. What\'s the difference?',
hw='A brilliant consultant is only as good as the briefing pack on their desk. Give them the wrong files, too many files, or files out of order, and the advice suffers, however good your question was.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **context engineering** is choosing what goes on the AI\'s desk (write-up 15): which instructions, documents, history and tool results, in what order.',
step='Prompt engineering (write-up 18) is about the wording of the request. Context engineering is about everything around it: retrieved documents (write-up 53), memory (write-up 67), tool results, examples, and what to leave out.\n\nIn agents, the desk changes every step, so deciding what to keep, summarise or drop becomes the main job. Too little context and the AI guesses; too much and it gets confused and costs more.',
met='AI assistants that pull the right files before answering. Coding tools that decide which parts of your code to show the AI.',
name='**Context engineering**.',
words=[("Context","everything the AI sees for this answer."),("Retrieval","fetching relevant information to include."),("Summarisation","shrinking older context to save space.")],
notit='It\'s not "stuff in everything". More context isn\'t better context. The skill is choosing the right, small set.',
take=["List what the AI sees for each answer; you might be surprised.","Remove what isn't needed before adding more.","Put the most important facts close to the question."],
close="The answer depends on what's on the desk.",
q="Which matters more: the question or the briefing pack?",
scene='A brilliant consultant, given the wrong files.\nThe advice suffers, however good the question.',
hook="Context engineering is choosing what goes on the AI's desk.",
extra="Not more context. The right context."),

dict(n=24, slug="structured-output", title="How Do You Get an AI to Fill In a Form?", topic="Structured output",
buzz='"Structured output." "JSON mode." Developers love it. For everyone else, it\'s why AI can now fill in forms reliably.',
hw='Ask an AI to read a receipt and you get a friendly paragraph. Your accounting system can\'t read a paragraph. It needs boxes: date, merchant, amount.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **structured output** means the AI answers in a fixed format, like a form with named boxes, instead of free text.',
step='You describe the form (called a schema): "date as a date, amount as a number, merchant as text". Many AI services can now guarantee the answer matches that form exactly, so software can use it directly.\n\nThe format being right doesn\'t mean the content is right. ₹1,180 can still be read as ₹118 (Series 2 covers exact data).',
met='Apps that turn a photo of a receipt or ID card into filled-in fields. AI features that create calendar events from emails.',
name='**Structured output**, often as **JSON**, defined by a **schema**.',
words=[("Schema","the description of the boxes and their types."),("JSON","a common text format for structured data."),("Field","one named box, like \"amount\".")],
notit='A perfectly shaped answer can still be wrong inside. Check the values, not just the format.',
take=["Ask for a fixed format whenever software will read the answer.","Validate the values: dates, totals, IDs.","Send unclear cases to a person instead of guessing."],
close="Boxes, not paragraphs.",
q="Where would a form-filling AI save you time?",
scene='A friendly paragraph about your receipt.\nYour accounting system needs: date, merchant, amount.',
hook="Structured output makes the AI fill in boxes instead of writing paragraphs.",
extra="Right format, still check the values."),

dict(n=25, slug="streaming", title="Why Does the AI Type Its Answer Word by Word?", topic="Streaming",
buzz='Chatbot answers appear word by word, like someone typing. Is it for show?',
hw='Order a three-course meal. Would you rather wait 30 minutes for everything at once, or get the starter in 2 minutes while the rest is cooked?\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **streaming** sends each piece of the answer the moment it\'s ready, instead of waiting for the whole thing.',
step='An AI writes one token at a time (write-up 11). Without streaming, you\'d stare at a spinner until the last token is done. With streaming, the first words arrive in about a second and you start reading.\n\nThe total time is about the same; it just feels much faster. The time until the first word appears is a key speed measure (write-up 52).',
met='Every chatbot that "types" its reply. Voice assistants that start speaking before they\'ve finished thinking.',
name='**Streaming** responses.',
words=[("Streaming","sending output piece by piece as it's made."),("Time to first token","how long until the first words appear."),("Server-sent events","a common web technique for streaming.")],
notit='Streaming doesn\'t make the AI think faster, and it isn\'t typing for show. It\'s real: you\'re seeing the answer as it\'s written.',
take=["Stream long answers in your AI apps; it feels much faster.","Measure time to first word, not just total time.","Don't stream where the whole answer must be checked first."],
close="Serve the starter while the main course cooks.",
q="Would you rather wait, or watch it arrive?",
scene='Wait 30 minutes for all three courses at once?\nOr get the starter now?',
hook="Streaming serves each word of the answer as soon as it's ready.",
extra="Same total time, feels much faster."),
]
